["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"service-sdk","stream_identity":"01a0cabb-b0b0-7507-816e-f5963e5d4c1d","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"aep.entity","fields":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"MIG2bd4d2baa92809e49541b148c846d4c17cb2effa7ad3398f717d36418ae77a5e","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/preserve-nested-optional-types","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":5,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"## Context\n\nGenerated Rust type rendering strips `Optional<T>` nested inside a list but does not retain its Option wrapper, so `List<Optional<String>>` becomes `Vec<String>` instead of `Vec<Option<String>>`. The generated binding therefore contradicts the ESS type even though top-level Optional values are correct.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nGenerated Rust recursively preserves Optional at every type nesting level, with focused coverage for `List<Optional<String>>` and top-level Optional/List combinations. Existing scalar, list, and top-level optional bindings remain unchanged.\n","relations":[{"derived_from":"story:optional-projection-fields"},{"serves":"vision:composable-services"}],"scope":[{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/service-builder/src/realization.rs"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/service-builder/tests/build.rs"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"Generated Rust must retain Optional wrappers inside collection shapes.","title":"Preserve nested Optional types in Rust bindings"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"}},"id":"MIG2bd4d2baa92809e49541b148c846d4c17cb2effa7ad3398f717d36418ae77a5e","lifecycle_state":"recorded","revision":5,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["aep.entity","MIG2bd4d2baa92809e49541b148c846d4c17cb2effa7ad3398f717d36418ae77a5e"]}]